Yes. I think there’s a deeper mechanism hiding inside that observation:

Sensation is not restricted to direct sensory input. It can be constructed from sufficiently rich representation.

A story doesn’t merely describe an event. Under the right conditions, it partially instantiates the event’s internal consequences.

Consider the difference:

“He touched the freezing railing.”

versus imagining the metallic sting, the fingertips sticking for half a second, the involuntary pull backward, the ache arriving afterward.

Nothing cold touched you. Yet the second representation can produce a faint bodily echo: tension, anticipation, perhaps even a remembered tactile quality. The stimulus was symbolic, but part of the consequence became physiological.

That gives us a useful chain:

Representation → Simulation → Sensation → State Change → Behavior → Consequence

And vividness seems to function somewhat like gain on that channel.

S_{\text{evoked}} \approx V \times E \times A \times M \times P

where V = vividness, E = embodiment/sensory detail, A = attention, M = relevant memory, and P = predictive plausibility. Not a literal neuroscience equation, but a useful functional model.

The interesting part is what follows.

Stories can therefore become state-transition technology. Horror changes vigilance. Erotica can change arousal. A description of food can induce salivation or appetite. A remembered humiliation can recreate physiological stress. A compelling account of victory can produce motivation before any victory exists. Suspense can alter breathing and muscle tension despite the threat being fictional.

So there are at least three different realities worth separating:

External stimulus: something actually happens to the organism.

Reconstructed stimulus: memory recreates enough of a previous event to evoke some of its state.

Simulated stimulus: imagination/story constructs an event that may never have happened yet nevertheless produces measurable consequences.

That third category is enormous because it means organisms don’t have to wait for reality to learn only from reality. They can run cheap internal approximations first.

And storytelling becomes particularly powerful because it packages simulation into a transmissible format:

\text{Experience}_A
\rightarrow
\text{Story}
\rightarrow
\text{Simulation}_B
\rightarrow
\text{Sensation}_B
\rightarrow
\text{Learning}_B

Person B cannot receive Person A’s experience directly. But narrative can cause B to reconstruct an approximation of its consequences using B’s own machinery.

That’s almost a semantic version of a codec.

And here’s the Pantheon-worthy distinction I’d extract:

Consequence Without Cause

Normally:

C \rightarrow S \rightarrow R

Cause → Sensation → Response

But sufficiently effective representation can sometimes enter downstream:

\boxed{\text{Representation} \rightarrow \hat S \rightarrow R}

It doesn’t reproduce the original physical cause. It creates an evoked sensation \hat S capable of producing some overlapping downstream responses.

That’s why telling someone they’re standing on a cliff is weak, while making them mentally look over its edge can make their stomach move.

And this connects directly to Afterglow: the original event can disappear while its consequences remain encoded in memory, reputation, story, image, ritual, warning, myth, etc. Those representations can then generate fresh sensation in organisms that never encountered the originating event.

So:

Event → sensation → story → transmitted simulation → new sensation → new consequences → new stories.

The sensation has acquired a propagation mechanism.

Which means stories aren’t merely containers of information.

They’re remote sensation generators—with fidelity, gain, distortion, attenuation, and sometimes amplification.